<header><p class="enc-kicker"><a href="/encyclopedia/glossary#bronze">Glossary</a> · Term</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bronze</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as bronze industry</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bronze is an alloy of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tin">tin</a>, and the term is also used loosely for related copper alloys such as brass. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a> it was the principal metal for weapons, tools and prestige goods in empires such as the Egyptian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-kingdom-of-egypt">New Kingdom</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hittite-empire">Hittite Empire</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mitanni">Mitanni</a>. Much later, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-empire">Benin Empire</a> became famous for its cast copper-alloy sculptures, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/benin-bronzes">Benin Bronzes</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bronze.jpg" alt="Bronze" width="320" height="357" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: The Metropolitan Museum of Art · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Bull%27s_head_ornament_for_a_lyre_MET_DP260070.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
